      "text": "To find the simplified value of the fourth term of the sequence, we need to understand the general form of the terms in the sequence. The sequence is defined as the sum of consecutive powers of 3:\n\n\\[\na_n = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + \\cdots + 3^{n-1}\n\\]\n\nThis is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = 3^0 = 1\\) and common ratio \\(r = 3\\). The sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\nS_n = a \\frac{r^n - 1}{r - 1}\n\\]\n\nIn our case, \\(a = 1\\), \\(r = 3\\), and \\(n = 4\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\na_4 = 1 \\cdot \\frac{3^4 - 1}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3^4 - 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(3^4\\):\n\n\\[\n3^4 = 81\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression for the fourth term becomes:\n\n\\[\na_4 = \\frac{81 - 1}{2} = \\frac{80}{2} = 40\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified value of the fourth term of the sequence is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{40}\n\\]",
